[size=5][b]MO2 Modlist Auto Sort[/b][/size]

[b]Sorts your left pane into a working order — and shows you what it changed, and why, before anything moves.[/b]

[line]

[size=4][b]What it does[/b][/size]

LOOT sorts your [i]plugins[/i] — the right pane, your ESPs. Nothing sorts your [i]mods[/i] — the left pane, where the actual files win or lose. That is done by hand, by dragging, using knowledge you either have or do not.

This plugin does that pass for you. It reads your mod list, works out what belongs above what, and reorders it.

Then — and this is the part that matters — it [b]tells you what it did[/b]. Every move, with the reason behind it, and the option to say no.

[line]

[size=4][b]How it decides[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]What kind of mod it is.[/b] Textures, meshes, animations, patches and so on each have a place in the stack. Fixes go near the bottom so they land on top of what they fix.
[*][b]What mods say they need.[/b] It reads the Requirements list off each mod's Nexus page. If A requires B, that is a real relationship and it is used as one.
[*][b]What actually collides.[/b] It looks at which mods overwrite the same files, because two mods that never touch each other do not need an opinion about their order.
[*][b]What you already decided.[/b] Your own choices are remembered and are never quietly overruled on a later sort.
[/list]

[line]

[size=4][b]It will not surprise you[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Nothing moves without a backup.[/b] Every sort writes a restorable snapshot first.
[*][b]Tools → Undo / Restore Mod List[/b] puts the previous order back.
[*][b]Conflicts it cannot settle are brought to you[/b] rather than guessed at.
[*][b]"The list is already in order"[/b] is a perfectly normal result. It does not move things to look busy.
[/list]

[line]

[size=4][b]Requirements[/b][/size]

[b][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/site/mods/]MO2 Nexus API Extender[/url][/b] — this is how the sorter reads mod requirements from Nexus without asking you for a key of its own.

Without it the sorter still works, using its own connection, and the report tells you which one it used. What you lose is the shared cache and the protected key.

[line]

[size=4][b]Install[/b][/size]

[list=1]
[*]Install the MO2 Nexus API Extender first.
[*]Download this archive.
[*]Extract it into your Mod Organizer 2 [b]plugins[/b] folder, so that a folder called [b]mo2_dag_sorter[/b] sits alongside the other plugins.
[*]Restart MO2.
[*][b]Tools → Auto-Sort Left Pane[/b].
[/list]

Right-clicking a selection of mods sorts [i]just those[/i], and re-checks them against Nexus rather than trusting what it already had.

[line]

[size=4][b]Read this before your first sort[/b][/size]

[b]This is beta software that reorders your mod list.[/b] It backs up first and it can undo, but a mod list represents real hours of your life. Do a sort when you have time to look at the report properly, not five minutes before you wanted to play.

The first sort on a large list takes a while — it is asking Nexus about several hundred mods. After that the answers are cached and it is quick. Requirements are re-checked once a week, because that is the part of a mod page authors actually edit after release.

[line]

[size=4][b]Honest limits[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Requirements are not load order.[/b] A mod requiring another tells you it must be installed, not that it must lose to it. The sorter treats it as one signal among several, not as the answer.
[*][b]Some requirements are invisible to it.[/b] Mods that list requirements per-file rather than per-mod return nothing through the API, so those relationships are not seen.
[*][b]It does not know your intentions.[/b] If you deliberately put a mod somewhere odd, tell it once and it will remember.
[/list]

[line]

[size=4][b]Source[/b][/size]

